Rather than trigonometry, it is well known that <imath>\frac{CP}{PR} = \frac{\phi}{1}</imath> due to the golden triangle. Since both pentagons are regular, they are similar. Therefore the ratio of the areas is equal to the ratio of the radii squared (rather than using the ratio of the sides of the pentagon). Let <imath>x</imath> be the area of the smaller pentagon. Note that we are given the larger pentagon has area <imath>2\phi</imath>. 

We will also use a well known identity of the golden ratio: <imath>\frac{1}{\phi} = \phi - 1</imath> (and for completeness sake for any student reading this in the future looking for other golden ratio identities: <imath>\phi^2 = \phi + 1</imath>) to simplify expressions involving the golden ratio rather than to do the algebra on the square roots. Then:

<cmath>
\begin{align*}
\frac{x}{2\phi} &= \left(\frac{PR}{CP}\right)^2 \\
\frac{x}{2\phi} &= \left(\frac{1}{\phi}\right)^2 \\
\frac{x}{2} &= \frac{1}{\phi} \\
\frac{x}{2} &= \phi - 1 \\
x &= 2\phi - 2 \\
x &= 2\left(\frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}\right)-2 \\
x &= 1+\sqrt{5}-2 \\
x &= \sqrt{5}-1
\end{align*}
</cmath>

